// number of lines, so a child keeps their place when switching languages). Made with tools/tales_en/*.json.
window.BK_TALES_EN = [{"id":"csillagtallerok","title":"The Star Coins","src":"Brothers Grimm, told by Benedek Elek","lines":["Once upon a time there was a little girl.","This little girl had no father","and no mother, and she was left as poor in this world as a church mouse.","She did not even have a little house to live in, nor a little bed","to lie down in; she had nothing but her clothes,","just the ones she wore, and a single piece of bread:","even that was given to her by a kind-hearted man.","With nothing else to do, the little girl set off, hoping the good Lord would help her.","As she walked and walked across the meadow, a poor man came up to her and begged:","\"Good little girl, give me something to eat. I have not had a bite for three days.\"","The little girl did not think twice; she gave her bread to the poor man.","\"God bless you,\" said the poor man gratefully, and went on his way.","The girl went on too, and a child came up to her, crying bitterly, and begged:","\"Give me something to cover my head with, my head is so cold!\"","Without a word, the little girl gave the child her hat.","She walked on and on, and again a child came up to her","who had not a single piece of clothing on.","She did not wait to be asked; she gave the child her little coat.","Then she came into the forest.","Evening was just falling, and a child came up to her and asked","for her little shirt, or else the cold would surely be the end of him.","The little girl said to herself:","\"It is dark anyway, no one can see. You can give away your shirt.\"","And so she gave it.","Now she truly had nothing at all.","There she stood, standing still, and looked up at the starry sky,","she sighed a deep sigh, and listen to this wonder! one by one the stars fell,","thick as rain, and when they touched the ground they all became pure white silver coins.","And from the sky there fell a lovely linen shirt too,","so white and so fine that it was a wonder to see.","She gathered up the coins, and from then on she had no more worries: she lived happily.","Tomorrow the little girl shall be your guest."]},
{"id":"var_es_forras","title":"The Castle and the Spring","src":"Hungarian tale, told by Benedek Elek","lines":["Two fairy maidens were always arguing over which of the two was more beautiful.","They were both so alike and so beautiful","that no one could ever choose between them.","\"Well then,\" said one fairy to the other, \"let us settle it another way.","Let us see which of us can make the more beautiful work!","She will be the more beautiful one, too.\"","One fairy built a wonderful castle on a mountain called Tartod.","It shone and sparkled with gold, silver and diamonds.","It had nine hundred windows, and it turned round and round on its heel.","And what did the other fairy do?","She made a spring burst out at the foot of the mountain.","A spring with healing power.","When both had finished their work, an old traveller came by.","They asked him to be the judge and decide which work was more beautiful.","\"It is my castle, isn't it?\"","asked the fairy who built the castle.","But the traveller, who was very thirsty, drank from the spring instead of answering,","and at once his thirst was gone, and he felt so well, as if he had grown young again.","\"This spring is the more beautiful,\" he said then, when he saw that","the castle fairy wanted more than a silent answer, \"it is finer because it is better.\"","The castle fairy grew angry, terribly angry.","In her great rage she turned to the mountain and said:","\"Well, mountain, I made you beautiful,","but you shall keep your beauty no longer.\" (Since then the mountain is called Tartod,","which is one of the high mountains of the Szekely Land.)","Then she pulled out the cornerstones of the castle, and of the gleaming,","shining castle not a trace was left; it vanished from the face of the earth."]},
{"id":"jakab","title":"Jakab and the Oats","src":"Hungarian tale, told by Benedek Elek","lines":["I will tell you a tale about little Jakab, so listen!","Once upon a time, beyond the far beyond, where the short-tailed piglet digs,","there lived a farmer, and he had a little servant lad named Jakab.","One day the farmer said to Jakab: \"Go, my son Jakab, to the field and cut the oats!\"","Jakab went, but the little rascal did not cut the oats.","No, he did not cut them, and he did not even poke his nose back home.","The farmer got angry and sent the puppy to bite Jakab.","Well, the puppy did not bite Jakab, and Jakab did not cut the oats.","So the farmer sent the stick to beat the puppy.","But the stick did not beat the puppy,","the puppy did not bite Jakab, and Jakab did not cut the oats.","The farmer got even angrier, and he sent the fire","to burn the disobedient stick.","But the fire did not burn the stick, the stick did not beat the puppy,","the puppy did not bite Jakab, and Jakab did not cut the oats.","\"Oh, you good-for-nothings!\" cried the farmer. \"Just you wait!\"","At once he sent the water to put out the fire.","Oh, but the water did not put out the fire, the fire did not burn the stick,","the stick did not beat the puppy,","the puppy did not bite Jakab, and Jakab did not cut the oats.","\"Is that so?!","Just you wait!\"","At once he sent the bull to drink up the water.","But the bull did not drink up the water,","the water did not put out the fire, the fire did not burn the stick,","the stick did not beat the puppy,","the puppy did not bite Jakab, and Jakab did not cut the oats.","Hmm, hmm, what should he do, what should he do?!","Aha, he had it!","He sent the butcher to slaughter the bull.","The butcher went, but he did not slaughter the bull,","the bull did not drink up the water, the water did not put out the fire,","the fire did not burn the stick, the stick did not beat the puppy,","the puppy did not bite Jakab, and Jakab did not cut the oats.","Well, that was all the farmer needed:","he sent the magistrate to have the butcher whipped.","And the magistrate had the butcher whipped, the butcher slaughtered the bull,","the bull drank up the water, the water put out the fire, the fire burned the stick,","the stick beat the puppy, the puppy bit Jakab,","and Jakab cut the oats at once, so well that not a single stalk was left.","If just one stalk of oats had been left, my tale would have gone on longer too."]},
{"id":"oregek","title":"The Old Folks","src":"Hungarian tale, told by Benedek Elek","lines":["I will tell you a tale about old folks, children, so listen!","Once a soldier, home from the army, was walking and walking on his way home,","and as he went through a village, he saw","a white-haired old man sitting out by the gate,","crying so bitterly that the whole village rang with it.","The soldier asked him:","\"Why ever are you crying, grandad?\"","\"Oh, how could I not cry, my dear son, when my father has given me a beating.\"","\"You don't say!","Why, you must be a hundred years old yourself. Surely your father is not alive?\"","\"Well, if you don't believe me, son, go into the yard.","Look, my father is chopping wood right now!\"","The soldier went in, and there truly was an even older man,","chopping wood better than any young lad could have.","The soldier greeted him:","\"Good day to you, grandad!","However could you have the heart to beat your son?\"","\"Why should I not have beaten him, when he did not obey his grandfather!\"","\"Well, well, what are you saying?!","So your father is still alive too?\"","\"Of course he is!","Just go into the house, he is sitting by the stove, smoking his pipe.","Go and see, if you don't believe me!\"","The soldier went in.","And there truly was the old man, sitting by the stove.","He was puffing big clouds of smoke from his pipe and grumbling about his grandson,","that if the boy disobeyed him just once more, he would really give it to him.","The soldier greeted the old man and asked:","\"Tell me, dear old grandfather, how old might you be?\"","\"Oh, my dear son, that I do not know.","But go to the priest who baptised me.","He will look it up in the parish book.\"","\"Come now, grandad, don't joke. Surely the priest who baptised you is not still alive?\"","\"He is, by God, so help me Christ, he is alive, son.","If you don't believe me, go and see!","You will surely find him at home, he is always reading the Bible.\"","The soldier went to the priest.","And there he truly was, sitting at the table.","In front of him lay the old Bible.","On his nose was a big pair of spectacles, and he was mumbling and reading from it.","\"A good and happy day to you, reverend father!\"","\"Welcome, my dear son!","What brings you here?","Are you perhaps getting married?\"","\"Not now, reverend father,","but I would like to know how old such-and-such an old man might be.\"","\"Well, my dear son, I cannot know that without the book,","but I will look it up in the parish book at once.\"","The priest stood up.","He went to his bookcase to take out the parish book.","But oh!","The bookcase was locked.","\"Well, my dear son,\" said the priest, \"I am very sorry,","but I cannot take out the parish book, because the key is not at home.","My mother took it with her to the vineyard!\"","If the priest's mother had not taken the key, my tale would have gone on longer too.","Here is the end, run off with it!"]},
{"id":"peterke","title":"Peterke","src":"Hungarian tale, told by Benedek Elek","lines":["Once there was a poor man.","This poor man had as many children as a sieve has holes, and one more.","The poor fellow did not even know whom to ask to be godfather to his newborn son,","because even the priest and the cantor were already godfathers to his children.","Oh, what a poor world, what a hard bed!","So the poor man set off with many sighs to look for a godfather.","By evening he reached a great forest, and he got so lost in it","that he could not find his way out at all.","He wandered this way and that, and all at once he met a tiny little man,","whose beard was two yards long and dragged along the ground behind him.","The poor man was frightened,","for he had never in all his life seen such a strange creature.","The tiny man spoke to him:","\"What are you doing here, friend?","What is your business?\"","The poor man was even more frightened.","\"Well, this must be the devil,\" he thought to himself,","\"and he will take me down, God knows where!\"","He answered him:","\"Oh, my dear tiny little man, my life and death are in your hands.","I set out to look for a godfather, because I have as many children","as a sieve has holes, and one more; the priest is already a godfather to them,","and the cantor too; not a single soul will agree to be a godfather.\"","The tiny man said:","\"Do not fret over that! I will be that child's godfather!","I ask only one thing: when he is seven years, seven months,","seven weeks and seven days old, give the boy to me, and I will take good care of him.\"","The poor man agreed.","He thought God had given him plenty of children; one given away would hardly be missed.","With that they turned around and went to the poor man's house,","and the child was baptised Peter.","His godfather at once bought him such baby clothes","that not even the king's child had finer ones.","Well, time went by.","All at once Peterke's parents noticed","that the time was nearly up when they had to give their child to the tiny man.","Oh! The poor man shed many bitter tears,","but his wife shed even more; they grieved and sighed a great deal","over why they had to part with this dear child of their hearts,","for there was no child so handsome, with black hair and black eyes, so clever-handed,","so wise as he was.","But all their grieving was in vain, in vain they shed so many bitter tears,","Peterke became seven years, seven months,","seven weeks and seven days old, and he had to be taken to his godfather.","The poor man said to his wife:","\"Well, wife, bake me a cake in the ashes. The time has come,","what I promised seven years ago, I cannot take back!\"","The poor woman cried, she cried bitterly, but she baked the cake all the same,","got her husband ready for the road, and he took Peterke away.","They walked and walked, and by evening they reached the great forest.","When they were deep inside, the earth opened before them, and out popped the tiny man.","He did not even let the poor man speak; he snatched Peterke from him,","and through the opening he had come out of, he vanished with him.","And the poor man set off home in great sorrow.","For fourteen whole days the tiny man carried his godson downwards,","and on the fourteenth day they reached a meadow","where golden grass grew, diamond flowers bloomed and golden horses grazed.","But, so I don't mix up my words, by then Peterke had grown into a fine, big lad.","For three days they went on through this land, across golden meadows everywhere,","through golden forests and past golden fish ponds.","On the third day they came to a castle that turned on a diamond leg.","Here the tiny man struck the castle gate with a golden rod,","the gate opened at once, and he himself turned a somersault over his head,","and lo, he became a young lady so wonderfully beautiful","that you could look at the sun, but not at her.","Peter could not say a word; he was so amazed he could not speak.","Then the beautiful young lady said:","\"Listen, handsome lad!","A wicked old witch put a curse on me that I should be a tiny man","until I found a bridegroom seven years, seven months, seven weeks and seven days old.","Now I have found you, and if you will have me, look, do you see this fine kingdom?","It will all be yours!\"","And Peterke had not a single word against that.","At once they harnessed six diamond-haired horses to a diamond coach,","drove up in a moment to Peter's family, brought down his father,","his mother and all his brothers and sisters to the land of happiness,","there they held a great feast, and they are still alive today, if they have not died."]},
{"id":"mindentudo","title":"Doctor Know-All","src":"Brothers Grimm, told by Benedek Elek","lines":["There was once a poor man, and his name was Crab.","One day this poor man took a cartload of wood into town and sold it to a doctor.","The doctor was just sitting at the table","when he paid for the wood, and the poor man nearly ate the fine food with his eyes","and the drink that was on the table.","He thought to himself: oh, how well off doctors are!","If only I could be a doctor, then I would live like a gentleman too,","I would eat and drink all day, and no one would be happier than me!","Whatever he thought, he went and asked the doctor:","\"Tell me, could I not become a doctor too?\"","\"Of course you could,\" answered the doctor.","\"Nothing is easier.","First of all, buy an ABC book, that one,","you know, the one with a rooster painted at the front;","sell your ox and your cart, and with the money buy doctor's clothes and everything","that a doctor needs.","Above all, do not forget to buy spectacles.","Then you will look like a real doctor.","Then have a big sign made, and have this written on it:","Here lives the Know-All Doctor. And nail this sign to your gate.\"","The peasant went home and did everything, to the last letter, as the doctor advised.","And soon the fame of the Know-All Doctor spread across seven times seven lands,","and even a lame step beyond that.","Word went everywhere that Master Crab knew everything,","that there was no secret in the world he could not find out.","No, there was none.","Once it happened that a great lord's money was stolen.","When it could not be found anywhere, someone told the great lord","that in such-and-such a place there was a know-all doctor,","called Crab; he should send for him, and he would tell where the money was.","Well, the great lord did not need telling twice; he had his horses harnessed,","drove to the know-all doctor, went in and asked if what they said about him was true.","\"Every word of it is true,\" said Master Crab.","They agreed at once, and Master Crab was taken to the lord's castle, and his wife too,","and there they were seated at once in the finest room,","the table was laid, and Master Crab and his wife sat down at it.","As they sat down, the first servant brought in a dish, and Master Crab nudged his wife.","\"Here is the first.\"","The servant nearly dropped the dish, he was so frightened.","He thought the know-all doctor meant: here is the first thief.","And indeed he was a thief, and so were the other servants.","The servant went out and said to the others: \"We are done for!","The doctor knows everything.","As I came in, he said to his wife: that is the first!\"","The second servant did not want to go in at all, but he had to go in all the same.","As he came in with the dish, Master Crab nudged his wife:","\"This is the second!\"","It was a wonder of God that the servant did not drop the dish, he was so frightened.","\"We are done for,\" he said outside, \"this man really does know everything.","When I came in, he said: this is the second.\"","The third one fared just the same.","The fourth servant had to carry in a covered dish; the lord went in too,","because he wanted to test whether the doctor knew what was in the dish.","Oh, how the poor doctor sweated,","he scratched his head, and in his misery he sighed: oh, poor Crab!","\"That's right,\" said the lord with great joy, \"there really is crab in it.","Now I believe that you know where the money is, too.\"","The lord went out, and the servants came at once, called out the doctor, surrounded him,","and confessed that the four of them had stolen the money;","they would give him a share, if only he did not give them away.","They even showed him where they had hidden the money.","With that Master Crab went back into his room.","The lord was there again and asked: well, what news, doctor?","Master Crab sat down at the table, took out his ABC book,","pushed his spectacles up on his forehead and said:","\"I will look it up in this book at once.\"","Meanwhile, so I don't forget, the fifth servant had hidden in the stove,","for he wanted to hear whether the doctor really knew everything.","Master Crab began to turn the pages back and forth, looking for the rooster picture.","For a long time he could not find the rooster, and he angrily struck the book.","\"I know you are in here, you must come out.\"","The fifth servant thought he was talking about him,","he jumped out of the stove in terrible fright and cried: He really does know everything!","Then Master Crab shut the book and led the lord to the place","where the servants had hidden the money,","and the lord paid him well, and the servants paid him well too,","he could hardly carry it all, he got so much money.","So it was, it is over, it was a tale.","Whoever does not believe it can go and see for himself."]},
{"id":"melyik","title":"Which Is Worth More?","src":"Hungarian tale, told by Benedek Elek","lines":["Over there, down a little, up a little, beyond the Tisza, beyond the Danube,","where the short-tailed spotted pig digs, there once was a king who had three sons.","When all three sons had grown up so much","that their moustaches began to sprout, the king said to them:","- Now, my dear sons, it is time for you to go and see the wide world!","The three princes set off on three fine horses, over hills and valleys,","through forests and fields, now downhill, now uphill, but always onward.","They went on and on, until one day they came to a huge city.","At the edge of the city they asked a boy:","- Hey, boy, what sort of town is this village?","The boy answered that this was the city of the White King.","The princes were glad to reach a king's city, and went straight to the palace,","and there before the king, and told him who they were and where they came from.","The White King was glad to see them, for he was good friends with their father.","But the princes were even gladder when they saw the White King's daughter.","They had seen many lovely girls at home, but none as lovely as this.","All three crowded around her and showed off, and when supper was over,","all three went into the king's room, and all three asked for his daughter's hand.","\"Now which one should I give her to?","the king wondered.","Oh, oh, if only I had two more daughters!\"","What could he do?","He did not want to make any of them angry, so he said:","- Listen, lads, I will not give my daughter to any of you now. Go on your way,","and whoever comes back a year from today with the most useful thing will have her.","Here is my hand on it, and my word is good.","The lads shook hands on it, and set off at dawn.","They rode together until they came to a great wild forest.","There the road split three ways, so they agreed to go three different ways,","and to meet again at this very spot in a year's time.","They parted in peace, and each went his own way.","All three lads travelled through many lands, and looked at everything closely,","they bought this and that, then threw it away when they found something better.","But time went by, and they had to turn back,","and in the end each of the three settled on one thing,","and by the time the year was up,","they were back in the great forest where they had parted.","Of course they wanted to see at once what each had brought.","The eldest said:","- Look, I bought a spyglass that lets you see to the end of the world.","The middle one said:","- I bought a cloak that you only have to wrap yourself in,","and it flies you in the blink of an eye wherever you tell it.","The third one said:","- And I bought an orange, and if a dead person,","not yet dead for twenty-four hours,","has it held under their nose, they wake up at once.","As they told each other this, the eldest looked into his spyglass,","and, dear Lord, he dropped it from his hand in fright.","- Did you see something bad?","asked his brothers.","- Oh, don't even ask, the princess is lying dead!","The other two looked into the spyglass too,","and it was true: the lovely princess was lying dead.","- Quick, quick, cried the middle prince, get under my cloak,","we will be there at once, and maybe she can still be brought back to life!","They got under the cloak, and hop! they were already in the king's palace.","The youngest prince took out the orange and held it under the princess's nose,","and look, at once her eyes flew open and her lips smiled!","Oh, how happy everyone was.","They were, but not for long.","For the princes began to quarrel over the girl.","The eldest said:","- She belongs to me, for I saw that she was dead.","The middle one said:","- You could have seen it all you liked:","without my cloak we would still be in the forest.","The girl belongs to me.","- Is that so?","said the youngest.","- And what good would it have been that one of us saw her","and the other flew us here, without my orange?","The king could not judge between them, so he had","to call together the wise men of the land, to let them judge.","They sat down together and talked for seven days and seven nights,","one said this, the other said that,","until on the seventh night they agreed after all","that the princess belonged to the youngest prince.","The older brothers accepted this too,","and danced at their brother's wedding so hard that the palace floor shook.","Then, in the middle of all the fun, the eldest took out his spyglass","and peeked into it.","- Come here, come, he said to his brother with the cloak, look in, what do you see?","- I see, said he, two lovely girls, like two shining stars.","- Well, if you see them, let's get under the cloak and take a closer look!","They had hardly thought of it when they were already there.","And yes, those two girls were king's daughters too.","They asked for their hands, got them, and carried them off in the cloak.","They did not stop until they were home.","By then their younger brother had come home too, with his wife.","Now there was a truly merry wedding.","Even the lame kicked up the dust as best they could.","There were plates and bowls enough, little food and drink, yet all was well."]},
{"id":"so","title":"Salt","src":"Hungarian tale, told by Benedek Elek","lines":["Once upon a time there was an old king who had three lovely daughters.","The king already had one foot in the grave,","and he wished to see all three of his daughters married.","That would not have been hard, for he had three kingdoms,","so each of his daughters would get one kingdom.","But just as no three apples are the same, the three kingdoms were not the same either.","So one day the king said to his daughters","that he would give the finest kingdom to the one who loved him best.","He asked the girls one by one, starting with the eldest:","- Tell me, my dear daughter, how much do you love me?","- As the dove loves clean wheat, said the girl.","- And you, my dear daughter?","he asked the middle one.","- I love you, dear father, as one loves a breeze in the hot summer.","- Now I ask you, he turned to the youngest, tell me, how do you love me?","- As people love salt, dear father!","answered the little princess.","- What are you saying, you worthless child! snapped the king,","get out of my court, and out of my kingdom too!","I never want to see you again, if that is all you love me!","In vain the princess wept and begged, in vain she explained","how much people love salt, in every way -","there was no mercy and no pardon: the little princess had to go out into the world.","The little princess set off, weeping bitterly, and wandered into a great forest.","She could not find her way out, so she made her home in a hollow tree,","and picked strawberries, raspberries, blackberries, hazelnuts, and lived there alone.","One day, when a whole year had passed, the prince of the next kingdom came by,","and he caught sight of the princess among the raspberry bushes.","But the princess saw the prince too, and in great fright ran into the hollow tree.","The prince went after her and called in:","- Who is in here?","The princess hid in the hollow, trembling","like an aspen leaf, and she did not say a single word.","The prince asked again:","- Hey!","Who is in here?","Are you a person or a devil?","If a person, come out; if a devil, go down to the bottom of hell!","The princess still did not dare to speak.","The prince asked a third time:","- Hey!","Who is in here?","Speak!","Are you a person or a devil? For I will shoot in at once!","At this the princess was terribly frightened,","and she crept out of the hollow tree, sniffling and sobbing.","Her dress was ragged and dirty, and she was very ashamed,","and with bitter tears she told the prince who she was.","The prince liked the princess very much, for however ragged she was,","however dirty her dress, her face was lovely and sweet.","He took her hand very gently and led her home to his palace,","and there he dressed her in a rich gold dress","set with diamonds, and he did not wait two weeks, not even one, I think,","not even a day, maybe not even an hour: he sent for a priest, they were married,","and they held such a great wedding that, well ... who could say how great it was.","Time went by, and the young couple lived together in peace,","and loved each other like two doves.","One day the king said:","- Now, wife, when I first saw you,","I did not really ask why your father had sent you away.","Tell me the plain truth!","- My dear husband, said the queen,","I can only tell you now what I told him then.","My father asked how I loved him, and I said: as people love salt.","- Very well, said the king, I will do something,","and I know your father's heart will turn back to you.","How he would turn it back, he did not tell his wife,","but he went into the other room,","wrote a letter to the old king, and asked him to come to lunch.","The letter went the next day, and on the third day the old king came in a coach and six.","The young king led the old king up into the palace,","into the finest room of the palace, where the table was already laid for two.","They sat down at the table, and the servants brought one fine dish after another,","but so that I do not mix up my words,","the young king had given orders","that every dish for the old king be cooked separately,","and baked, and that no salt be put in it.","Well, that was some lunch!","The old king tasted the soup, took one spoonful, then two,","but put the spoon down at once: he could not eat the soup, it had no salt at all.","The old king thought to himself: they must have forgotten the salt in this,","but there will surely be some in the boiled beef.","There was not even a poppy seed's worth in that.","They brought in the roasts one after another, but they could take them back again,","for the old king only gave each a little lick","and did not bite into it, so bland and salt-free was every costly roast.","This the old king could not bear without a word.","- Listen, my young friend, what kind of cook do you have, who cooks without salt?","- He always cooks with salt at other times, Your Majesty,","but I heard that Your Majesty does not love salt,","so I ordered that his head would be cut off if he put even a poppy seed of salt in.","- Well, my young friend, you did wrong there, for I love salt very much.","Who told you that I do not love it?","- Why, your very own daughter, Your Majesty, said the young king.","At that very moment the door opened,","and in came the queen, the old king's youngest daughter.","Oh, dear God, how glad the old king was!","For long ago he had come to regret, with all his heart, sending his daughter away,","and since then he had had her searched for all over the world.","And so, of course, he now gave his youngest daughter his biggest kingdom.","The young king took this kingdom into his care as well,","and they are still alive today, if they have not died."]},
{"id":"het_hollo","title":"The Seven Ravens","src":"Brothers Grimm, told by Benedek Elek","lines":["Once there was a man who had seven sons.","This man always prayed that God would bless him with a little daughter too.","God heard his many prayers, and gave him a lovely little baby girl.","But the little girl was so weak when she was born","that they feared she would not live even one day.","Quickly they sent the seven sons to fetch fresh spring water from the forest,","to christen the child with it, so that she would not die unchristened.","The boys ran into the forest, but when they reached the spring,","each wanted to be the first to fill the jug, and the jug fell into the well.","In it went, and because the well was deep, they could not get the jug out.","The poor boys were sad, not knowing what to do now.","They did not dare go home, so they stayed in the forest,","and their father could not imagine what had happened to them.","- They must have started playing, he said, and forgotten all about the water.","Still the boys did not come, and their father grew angrier and angrier.","In his great anger,","that now the little girl would die unchristened, he began to curse:","- I wish all seven of them would turn into ravens!","The words had hardly left his mouth when he heard loud chattering and cawing.","The man looked up, and there were seven black ravens flying above him.","Oh, dear God, a great heavy sorrow fell upon the man's heart,","and an even greater one upon his wife's heart.","They wept and wailed and tore their hair, but no matter: their sons stayed ravens.","Still, they were lucky that the little girl did not die,","but grew stronger and prettier from day to day.","For a long time the little girl did not know that she had brothers,","but one day she happened to hear from the neighbours","that she had had seven brothers and they had become ravens because of her.","Oh, dear God, how sad this made the little girl!","She ran to her father and mother and asked:","- Is it true, my dear parents, that I had seven brothers?","Where are they, what became of them?","- Well, there is no denying it, said her father, you had seven brothers,","but the poor boys were turned into ravens when you were born.","We will weep for them until we die.","From that moment on the little girl had no peace.","She felt her brothers became ravens because of her, and she made a vow -","come what may -","that she would not rest","until she had found her brothers and freed them from the curse.","She told her parents what she meant to do,","but oh, how they wept and wailed then!","Was it not enough that they had lost seven dear, handsome sons,","must they now lose their only daughter too?","But all their weeping was in vain, the little girl could not stay at home.","She had a little ring her parents had given her to remember them, she took only that,","and a piece of bread and a jug of water: and so she set out into the world.","On and on went the little girl, over hills and valleys, through forest","and across fields, on and on she went, until she came to the very end of the world.","But there at the end of the world the sun shone so hot","that the little girl ran on in terror: she thought she would burn to ashes on the spot.","She ran to the moon, but it was so cold that she nearly froze.","The poor thing ran on in a panic and did not stop until she reached the stars.","They twinkled and smiled so kindly, sat her down among them, and asked what she wanted,","how she had come here, and when she told them","that she was looking for her brothers, the Evening Star said:","- Well, you have come to the right place, little girl.","Your seven brothers are on the Ice Mountain.","Here, I will give you a little key,","with it you will open the Ice Mountain gate, and there you will find your brothers.","She thanked them nicely for the gift,","said goodbye to the stars, and ran up the Ice Mountain like the swift wind.","At the top she looked and looked for the key to open the door, but could not find it.","Oh, dear God, what could she do now?","How could she free her brothers?","She thought and thought, then took her little knife and cut off her little finger,","put it into the gate, and look, at that very moment it opened.","She went through the gate, but after two steps a dwarf stood before her and asked:","- What are you looking for here, little girl?","The little girl answered:","- I am looking for my brothers, the seven ravens, dear dwarf.","Are they here?","- Here, yes, said the dwarf, but they are not at home just now.","But if you want to wait for them until they come home, then come in.","They went into the seven ravens' room, and there the dwarf laid the table,","set out seven plates, seven cups, food on all seven plates, drink in all seven cups.","The little girl tasted the food of all seven, one by one,","she ate a little from each of the seven plates,","she drank from the cups too, and into the last cup she dropped her ring.","All at once the little girl heard a great flapping of wings","and cawing, and the dwarf announced:","- Now, little girl, here come the raven lords!","And in came the ravens, flying through the door,","they sat down at the table and began to eat,","but they saw at once that someone had eaten their food and drunk their drink.","- Who has eaten and who has drunk here? they asked each other.","It was a person, that is certain.","Then the youngest raven happened to look at the bottom of his cup, and saw the ring.","- Look, a ring! he cried.","This was our parents' ring!","Look!","- So it is!","This is our parents' ring! they all cried in great wonder.","- Oh, dear God, said the eldest,","if only our little sister were here, we would be freed from the curse at once!","At that very moment the little girl ran out from behind the door, where she had hidden,","and, hear this wonder, the seven ravens turned into boys at once!","And what handsome boys they were!","One by one they hugged and kissed the little girl, then without","a word more they set off home, and did not stop until they got there.","And then at home!","That was where the real joy was!","They are all still alive today, if they have not died."]},
{"id":"madarak","title":"The King of the Birds","src":"Brothers Grimm, told by Benedek Elek","lines":["Tale, tale, I tell a tale now about the birds, and the birds' terrible great battle...","It happened like this:","once the bear and the wolf were walking in the forest, and as they wandered about,","they heard a lovely, lovely bird song.","The bear stopped in wonder, opened his eyes and mouth wide, and listened to the song.","Then he asked the wolf:","\"Tell me, friend wolf, what bird is it that sings so beautifully?\"","\"That is the king of the birds,\" said the wolf.","\"Later we will go to him and pay our respects.\"","\"Good,\" agreed the bear, \"I have never seen a royal palace anyway. Let's go.\"","\"Oho, it is not as easy as that,\" said the wolf,","\"we must wait until the queen comes home.\"","A minute passed, then two, and the queen came,","carrying food in her beak; the king came too,","and his beak was full as well, and then they flew into a hollow in a tree:","that was the palace, I mean to say, the nest.","The bear wanted to climb up at once, but the wolf pulled him back:","\"Oho, stop.","Wait until they have both gone away.\"","Very well, the bear agreed, and they walked on,","but first they took a good look at the hollow of the tree where the nest was.","But the bear could not rest, and after a little while they went back to the tree.","By then the king and the queen had indeed flown out of the nest,","so the bear climbed up the tree, looked into the hole and saw","that there were five baby birds in the nest.","\"Is this a royal palace?\"","grumbled the bear.","\"Well, what a shabby palace it is! And are these royal children?","Why, they are just common peasant brats!\"","Oh, the little birds flew into a terrible rage at these insulting words.","Beside themselves, they shouted and chirped:","\"We are not peasant brats,","we are true royal children, you should know that, you grumbling bear!","Just you wait, you will be sorry for these words!\"","The bear was startled at this, and so was the wolf,","and they slunk back to their cave, deeply worried about what would happen now.","Meanwhile the king of the birds came home, and the queen too,","but they were nearly scared to death when they heard the children's terrible chirping.","The eldest spoke, and then the others after him:","\"The bear was here and he said","that we are not royal children, but common peasant brats.","Until we know what the real truth is, we will not move our beaks","or our wings, and we will starve to death right here in this place,","but we will not live in shame.\"","\"Now, now,\" said the king, shaking his head, \"what sort of talk is this?","My father was a king, and my grandfather, back to the seventy-seventh one.","Just calm down, and I will show that old peasant who and what I am.\"","He did not stay at home for even a blink of an eye,","he flew with his followers to the bear's cave and called in:","\"Listen, you old honey thief, how dare you insult my children?","Know that I declare war on you, and woe to you and all your kin!\"","Now this was no joke at all.","Friend bear got up and sent word to the four-legged animals to get on their feet,","because the king of the birds had declared war, and it would be such a war","as there had never been since the world began.","And it was good that they got on their feet,","because within an hour the king of the birds gathered every bird in the world,","and not only the birds, but even the beetles, the bees and the flies,","every kind of creature that had wings.","When the army had gathered, the king of the birds sent out the wren","to spy and find out who would be the leader of the enemy.","Away flew the wren,","hid under a leaf on a tree beside the bear's cave, and listened.","Along came the bear, very grumpy, growling and grumbling, called the fox and said:","\"Listen, fox, you are the cleverest of all the animals,","you must lead the army.\"","\"Good,\" said the fox, \"but what shall the signal be?\"","One said one thing, another said another,","at last the fox suggested something that everyone liked.","He said:","\"I have a fine, long, bushy tail, it will do well as a flag.","If I hold my tail up high, it means: follow me!","If my tail droops down, then: run away wherever you can.\"","The wren flew back at once and reported what he had heard.","\"Very well, come on then, friend fox,\" said the bird king, \"your tail will droop!\"","Early the next morning the battle began.","The four-legged animals set off with a terrible roar,","(the donkey was among them) with a dreadful braying,","(the ox was among them too), and heaven and earth rang and shook with it.","But the birds set off too, the air whirred and buzzed with the beating of their wings,","the forest rang and echoed, oh, what will happen here, Lord Jesus Christ!","Then the king of the birds at once sent out a few stag beetles,","and they flew straight at the fox,","one here, the other there, pinching and stabbing him with their horns,","the fox goes this way and that, holding his tail up high,","but only with pain and trouble, and the others rush after him,","then all at once the fox's tail droops down,","and off they go, run for your lives, the four-legged army fled head over heels,","as if they had all gone blind!","The king and the queen flew home at once and said with great joy:","\"Now, children, don't be sad, eat and drink, we have won the battle!\"","The children said:","\"Not until the bear comes here and says sorry to us.\"","\"The children are right,\"","said the king, and he flew at once to the bear's cave and called in:","\"Listen, you old honey thief, come before my nest and ask my children to forgive you,","or else I will put you to a terrible death.\"","Whether the bear liked it or not,","he slunk to the nest and, weeping, begged the royal children to forgive him.","And now the children ate and drank and were merry,","the forest rang with it, they sang like that all day long.","That's how it was!"]},
{"id":"molnarinas","title":"The Miller's Boy and the Cat","src":"Brothers Grimm, told by Benedek Elek","lines":["It was not here, it was not there, but somewhere it was:","there was once a mill, and in it a miller,","who had no wife and no children,","only two miller lads and a little apprentice boy with him.","One day the miller said to the lads:","\"Listen, lads, I have grown old, my place is now beside the fire.","Go out, and whichever of you brings me the best horse,","to him I will give the mill, and all I ask is that he look after me until I die.\"","The lads jumped at this offer, and they set off that very day,","not only the big lads, but even the little apprentice, though they mocked him a lot:","\"And you, what do you want?","Sit by the stove, stay at home,","you will never get a horse, not while the world lasts and two days more.\"","But let them talk and mock, the little apprentice, whose name was Jancsi,","went with them, stumbling after them over hill and valley.","By evening they reached a cave, went in and lay down, but the two lads only waited","for Jancsi to fall asleep, then they crept out of the cave and left Jancsi there.","In the morning Jancsi woke up, looked around, and saw no one,","and he grew very sad: what should he do now?","Well, he got up and felt his way out of the cave,","and set off all alone: come what may, perhaps God will be with him.","As he walked along on his own, a little spotted cat came towards him and spoke:","\"Where are you going, little Jancsi?\"","\"Don't even ask,\" said Jancsi, \"you can't help me anyway.\"","\"Can't I?","You see, I know what you wish for.","You want to get a fine horse.","Come with me, enter my service, and if you serve me faithfully for seven years,","I will give you a horse so fine that it has no match across seven plains.\"","\"Hm,\" thought Jancsi, \"this must be some magic cat, maybe it is telling the truth.\"","He did not think long, he took service with the cat and went to her little palace.","Oh, and how many cats there were, dear good God!","Hundreds of them jumped and frolicked up and down the stairs,","inside in the rooms, outside in the yard, everywhere.","There were so many that Jancsi's eyes could not take them all in.","And even more in the evening, when they sat down to the table.","They played music so that the palace rang with it, and when supper was over,","the little spotted cat sprang in front of Jancsi and called:","\"Come, Jancsi, dance with me.\"","\"No, I do not dance with a cat,\" said Jancsi, \"I have never done that in my life!\"","\"Then put him to bed,\" the little spotted cat ordered the others.","They gathered round him, led him to his room, pulled off his boots, put him to bed.","In the morning they came in again, dressed him and washed him,","and wiped his face with their little tails.","In a word, Jancsi had a good life.","He thought that since he had taken service, he ought to do some work too.","The little spotted cat gave him a silver axe,","a golden scythe and a diamond rake.","With the axe he chopped wood, with the scythe he cut grass for the horses (oh,","what horses there were in the stable!),","with the rake he raked up the hay, well,","no one could complain: if they kept him well, he worked for it too.","When the seven years were over, the little spotted cat called Jancsi into the stable:","there stood seven steeds in a row, all with golden coats, and she said to him:","\"Now, Jancsi, of the seven horses choose the one","that pleases your eyes and your heart the most.","Then go home, and in three days I will bring your horse after you.\"","Jancsi chose the most beautiful steed,","said goodbye to the little spotted cat and walked home nicely on foot.","He went home just as he had come, in the same clothes,","because the little spotted cat gave him nothing else:","she said he should just go home ragged and dirty, that would be best.","And ragged he was indeed!","In seven years his clothes had worn away so much","that only rags and tatters hung from his body.","And of course the lads were already at home: one had got a blind old nag,","the other a lame one, but no matter, they had got something after all.","Oh, how they laughed at Jancsi!","\"Didn't we tell you to stay at home?\"","Jancsi went into the mill and greeted the miller,","but the miller barely greeted him back, did not even let him sit down,","he chased him out of the house and said: there is the goose shed, let him live in that,","he would not sit at one table with such a ragged, dirty lad.","\"Fine,\" thought Jancsi, \"this will change yet,\" and he went to the shed without a word,","lay down there and slept like a log till morning.","That very morning it was three days","since he had left his service, and as the sun rose,","there came a golden coach with six horses, and beside it a groom with the seventh horse.","The coach stopped, and look, out stepped a beautiful, beautiful princess;","it was the little spotted cat, whom Jancsi had served for seven years.","She asked the miller:","\"Where is the little miller's boy?\"","The miller answered:","\"Out in the goose shed.","We don't let him into the house, he is so dirty, so ragged.\"","\"Then call him here,\" ordered the princess.","Meanwhile, from the coach they took out beautiful golden","clothes with diamonds, they washed Jancsi,","combed his hair with a golden comb, then dressed him in the golden","clothes with diamonds: not even a prince could look finer than Jancsi.","Then the princess wanted to see","what kind of horses the miller lads had brought.","Well, one was lame, the other blind.","\"Bring the seventh steed here,\" she ordered the servants.","\"Well, I have never seen such a fine horse in all my life!\" cried the miller.","\"If you haven't, then know that it belongs to the little miller's boy.\"","\"Then the mill is his too,\" said the miller.","\"My palace is his, not your mill,\" said the princess.","And with that she and Jancsi got into the coach and drove off to the little palace.","Little palace, did I say?","It had grown into a palace so big that it had seventy-seven windows,","of pure silver, its door was gold, its stairs were diamond.","There they held a great wedding at once, and danced for seven days and seven nights.","Tomorrow may they be your guests."]},
{"id":"hammas_jutka","title":"Ashy Jutka","src":"Hungarian tale, told by Benedek Elek","lines":["Once upon a time there was a poor widow.","This woman had only one daughter, but she did not have much joy even in her.","This girl was so lazy that word of it spread beyond seven counties.","From morning till evening she sat in the ashes, poking at them,","stirring them with her fingers, and the village folk named her Ashy Jutka.","Oh dear, oh dear, how the poor widow fretted!","Being a widow was not enough, her daughter had to be lazy too.","All her kind words were in vain: you should work too, my daughter,","at something; at least spin one spindle of hemp a day!","She could talk all she liked: in one ear and out the other.","Then one day the poor woman ran out of patience,","she grabbed her distaff, with both hands, and gave Jutka a sound beating.","\"There, take that!","Do you want more?\"","Oh, how Jutka cried, the whole village rang with her crying, and she told her mother","that she would not beat her any more, because she was going out into the world.","\"Go with God, and have a good road with no mud!\"","Jutka did go, but as she stepped out into the street crying loudly,","who should come along but the prince in a coach with six horses.","He stopped the coach and asked the poor woman:","\"Tell me, good mother, why did you beat your daughter?\"","The widow answered:","\"Because, if you please, I cannot get enough hemp for her,","she has already spun even the thatch of my garden shed, and she still wants to spin.","Too much of a good thing is too much!\"","\"Really, is this girl truly such a good spinner?\"","said the prince, \"then let her come to me, there is spinning for her there.","A great granary is full of hemp, and if she spins it all in one month,","here is my hand on it, I will make her my wife.\"","\"Well now,\" thought the woman, \"nothing will come of that,","but take her away!\" The girl thought just the same,","but she got into the coach all the same, in case some wonder of the world might happen.","When they arrived at the king's palace,","the prince at once took Jutka into the granary.","He said:","\"Now, Jutka, here you can spin as much as you like.","What I said, I said: if you spin in one month","all the hemp that is here, I will make you my wife.\"","Jutka could not even speak, she was so scared at the sight of that sea of hemp.","Why, she could not spin this in a thousand years.","She cried and grieved day and night.","One day passed after another, one week went by, then two,","then three, but not a single thread of the hemp was missing.","The little serving boy who brought her food asked when she would start spinning,","but Jutka only answered: there is still plenty of time for that.","But when she was alone in the granary, she cried like a rain shower.","How could she have been such a great fool as to sit down in the granary!","Soon the month would be over,","and the prince might even have her head cut off in his anger, for making a fool of him.","Just as she was thinking about this, all of a sudden,","good God knows from where, a teeny-tiny dwarf popped up in front of her.","But this dwarf had a beard seven yards long,","and wherever he went, he swept up the rubbish with his beard.","Jutka was scared: \"Oh God, help me, what an ugly beast, what an ugly little man!\"","But the dwarf spoke up:","\"Good evening, Ashy Jutka!","I know what your trouble is.","Don't worry a bit, in one week I will spin every last thread","of the hemp that is here, and even wind it on the reel.","But then you must come with me.\"","\"How can I go with you,\" said the girl, \"when the prince has chosen me for himself?\"","\"Very well,\" said the dwarf, \"I will not take you away if,","by the time I finish the spinning, you guess my name.\"","The girl thought: so be it.","It made no difference now which way her fate would turn.","She shook hands with the dwarf, and the bargain was made.","After that the dwarf came to the granary every night,","and he spun so fast that Jutka's eyes and mouth stood wide open in wonder.","What he spun by night he took away, and by day he reeled it in the forest branches.","When there was only one day left of the month,","the little serving boy came with supper, and Jutka asked:","\"So what news is there outside, little serving boy?\"","\"Well, the news is that if you spin the hemp by tomorrow evening,","you will be the prince's wife.\"","\"And didn't you hear anything else, little serving boy?\"","\"Yes, I did.","This afternoon I went hunting with the prince,","and as we wandered in the forest, we heard a clattering among the branches.","We looked up, and what did we see there?","A teeny-tiny dwarf with a beard seven yards long, just imagine!","He was reeling among the branches, and he kept saying:","I am Tirpe-Torpe Gyurka,","My wife will be Ashy Jutka.","The prince laughed loudly","at what that ugly little man was saying, and with that we went on.\"","Oh, how glad Ashy Jutka was to hear this!","Now she knew the dwarf's name.","The hemp would be spun, and still she would not have to go with the dwarf.","On the last evening the dwarf came and said to the girl:","\"Now, Jutka, I have spun the hemp to the last thread, now come with me.","Or do you perhaps know my name?\"","\"Of course I know it,\" said Jutka, \"your name is Tirpe-Torpe Gyurka.\"","In the morning came the prince, and the whole court with him.","Oh, how amazed they all were!","Everyone said that since the world began, no human eye had seen such a thing.","Even the old king said:","\"Well, my son, until now I was against your marriage,","but now I say: only the spade, the hoe and the great bell shall part you two.\"","They held the wedding right away.","And what a wedding it was!","Everyone was there who was not late.","Everyone ate and drank who got something.","Two old women especially pushed about in vain, everything was eaten up before them.","So they went off to the prince to complain.","But even the prince had never seen two such ugly women.","One's eyelids, and the other's lips, hung down to the ground.","He asked them one after the other:","\"Now why have your eyelids drooped down to the ground?\"","\"Because, my royal lord, I spun all my life,","and my eyes were always on the spindle.\"","\"And why have your lips drooped down?\"","he asked the other.","\"That is because I licked the hemp a lot, so that I could spin more.\"","Oh, how frightened the prince was!","Why, then even his lovely wife might grow ugly like that.","So at once he forbade Jutka","ever again in her life even to hold a spindle in her hand.","And Jutka, believe it or not, obeyed, and she lives still, if she has not died."]},
{"id":"aranyfonal","title":"The Golden Thread","src":"Brothers Grimm, told by Benedek Elek","lines":["Once upon a time there was a poor miller who had a beautiful daughter.","Where he went, which way he went, I do not know; enough that he met the king,","and the king spoke with him, asking who he was and how he was getting on.","Who knows what the miller was thinking? Surely he wanted the king to think more of him,","for he began to boast that he had a beautiful daughter,","as lovely as the shining star in the sky, but that was nothing,","for his daughter was the kind of girl who could spin straw into gold.","\"Well,\" said the king, \"I have never heard of such a thing in all my life.","Listen, miller, if your daughter is truly so clever,","bring her to my palace tomorrow, and let me see if your words are true.\"","The miller's lip drooped a little, for the girl was beautiful, that much","was true, but that she could spin straw into gold was a downright lie.","But he had said it, he could not take it back, and the next day he took her to the king.","He did not say a word to his daughter, he only led her into the palace,","then left her in God's care and ran out of the palace.","The girl went in to the king and greeted him politely,","the king received her, then without a word he led her into a room.","That room was full of straw, and the king said to the girl:","\"Here is the straw.","Here is the spinning wheel.","If you do not spin this straw into gold by morning, you shall die a dreadful death.\"","The poor girl could not even open her mouth before the king locked the door on her.","Oh dear God, the girl wept, full of worry and sorrow, for what was she to do now?","She could hardly even spin, let alone spin gold out of straw.","As she sat there crying to herself, all at once the door opened,","and in came a tiny old little man, who said:","\"Good evening to you, miller's pretty daughter!","Why are you crying so bitterly?\"","\"How could I not cry, my dear little man, when the king has ordered me","to spin all this straw into gold!\"","\"What will you give me if I spin it?\" asked the little man.","\"I will give you my red coral beads,\" said the girl.","The little man took the beads, then sat down at the spinning wheel; it whirred","and turned, and with every three turns a spool was full of golden thread.","Dawn had hardly broken, and not one stalk of straw was left:","all of it had turned into shining golden thread,","and by the time the king came in, there was no sign of the little man at all.","The king was amazed, and he thought to himself: if this girl is so clever,","let her spin even more, and he led her into another chamber.","That one was full of straw too, and he told the girl","to spin it all by morning, if she loved her life.","Then he locked the door, and the girl wept to herself,","but not for long, for the door opened, the little man came in and said:","\"What will you give me if I spin this straw too?\"","\"I will give you my gold ring, my dear little man!\"","The little man took the ring and by morning he had spun every last stalk of straw:","the whole chamber shone with golden thread.","In came the king, amazed and astonished, and said to the girl:","\"Now, girl, come with me to the third chamber.","It is full of straw too, and if you spin it into gold, I will make you my wife!\"","He led the girl into the third chamber,","locked the door on her, and the girl just wept and wailed bitterly.","But the door opened, the tiny old man came in and asked:","\"What will you give me if I spin this too?\"","\"I have nothing left, my dear little man!\" wailed the girl.","\"Then promise me that when you are queen, you will give me your first child.\"","The girl thought: who knows if that will ever happen; whatever will be,","will be. So she promised what the little man wished,","and he spun the straw by morning:","the king was nearly blinded by the dazzling shine when he came in that morning.","The king did not go back on his word,","he married the miller's daughter, and when a year had passed,","a lovely, beautiful child was born to the miller's daughter who had become queen.","She hardly thought of the little man, but he thought of her, and that very day he came:","\"Here I am, queen!","Give me what you promised!\"","Oh, how frightened the queen was! She promised him anything, a sea of treasure,","if only he would not ask for her child, but the little man said:","\"I do not want your sea of treasure, I want the child.\"","The queen wept so hard that the whole palace rang with it,","and the little man felt sorry for her and said:","\"Very well!","I will give you three days. If in that time you find out","what my name is, you may keep your child.\"","With that the little man went away,","and from that hour the queen racked her brains, over and over,","wondering what the little man's name could be.","She thought up all sorts of names, every which way,","and she also sent messengers all over the country","to gather every name they heard, for surely the little man's name would be among them.","The next day the little man came, and the queen said a great heap of names,","she said a hundred of them, but the little man just shook his head.","On the second day, when the little man came, the queen listed the strangest,","rarest names she could find, but she could list all she liked:","not one of them was the little man's name.","On the third day the messengers came back, but not one brought a new name.","But just as evening was falling and the sun was setting,","a weary young servant boy came and told the queen this:","\"My queen, Your Majesty, I have not heard a new name either,","but as I came through the forest, I saw a little house;","in front of the house a fire was burning, and around the fire danced","and hopped a funny little man, who kept shouting:","Paul knows well what he hoes and what he mows,","My name is Riki-Roki-Rokka Paul, Rokka Paul.","Tomorrow, tomorrow, the day after, oh what fun,","The king's own son, I swear it, will be mine, will be mine!\"","Oh, how glad the queen was when she heard this!","Why, that was her own little man!","Hardly a minute or two had passed when the little man came, full of confidence:","\"Well, queen, do you know my name?\"","The queen pretended not to know, and she tried out the strange name bit by bit:","\"Riki, is it?\"","\"No, no!\"","\"Roki, is it?\"","\"No, no!\"","\"Well then, it is Riki-Roki-Rokka Paul, is it not?\"","\"The devil told you that, no one else!\" the little man stamped in his rage.","He ran out of the palace and in his rage stamped down so hard he sank to his waist,","then he jumped out again, turned a somersault over his head,","and ran away so fast that no human eye ever saw him again.","If Riki-Roki-Rokka Paul had not run away, my tale would have lasted longer too."]},
{"id":"muzsikusok","title":"The Musicians","src":"Brothers Grimm, told by Benedek Elek","lines":["Once upon a time there was a man who had a donkey.","The donkey was old, and since he was of little use because of his age,","his master begrudged him even the thistles, and kept thinking how to get rid of him.","Though a donkey is a donkey,","he saw what his master meant to do and thought: I will run off before I am clubbed.","For oh, people are ungrateful, they are capable even of that!","Well, he did not think about it for long,","he said goodbye to the gatepost and set off. And where did he set off to?","Why, he set off straight for Brasso.","Well, why there?","Why?","Believe it or not, he had made up his mind to become a musician there.","He knew about that too, of course he did!","On and on went the donkey, and on the way he met a hound.","This hound was old too; he lay by the roadside sighing to himself:","oh, how old I have grown, oh, how weak I have become!","In the old days, when I could run well, what a good life I had!","The donkey stopped and asked:","\"Why are you sighing, why are you grieving, my friend the hound?\"","\"How could I not sigh, how could I not grieve, when I am old and cannot run!","My master has already passed sentence on me: he will shoot me, for I am no use to him.\"","\"Well now, if your master wants to shoot you, come with me.","I am going to Brasso to become a musician there; you can become one too.","You have a good voice, and surely you know how to play the drum as well,","you will see what a grand life you will have.","I will blow the trumpet, you will beat the drum: what fine music that will be!\"","\"There is sense in what you say, you have a clever tongue,\"","said the hound, and he joined the donkey: off he went with him to Brasso.","They had not gone even half an hour when they met an old cat.","The poor thing was staggering by the roadside, as gloomy as three days of rain.","\"And what is wrong with you, old whisker knight?\"","asked the donkey.","\"Oh, do not even ask,\" answered the cat.","\"I have grown old, my teeth have fallen out,","I cannot catch mice, and my mistress wants to drown me.","Is this what I deserve?","Oh, how she petted me when I caught mice by the hundred!\"","\"Never you mind,\" said the donkey.","\"Come along with us, come to Brasso to be a musician.","You know all about night music anyway,","you will have a life there like you have never had in all your days.\"","The cat did not think for a single minute; he shook hands with the donkey,","joined them, and now the three of them went on toward Brasso.","On and on they went, further and further,","then they came to a village, and there all at once they saw an old rooster","huddled sadly on top of a gate.","His head hung down, his wings drooped: anyone who saw him felt sorry for him.","\"Good day to you, knight of the spurs!","And what is wrong with you?","My, how low you have fallen!\"","\"How could I not, my dear brother donkey,","when my mistress expects guests tomorrow and wants to feed them my old flesh.\"","\"Oh, you silly thing,\" said the donkey, \"surely you will not let them kill you.","Come with us to Brasso, join our band.","You have a lovely voice: you are just the one we were missing!\"","Hop! The rooster fluttered down from the gate,","crowed a great crow, and marched off proudly with the band.","On and on they went, and by evening they came to a forest.","There they made camp at once: the donkey and the hound lay down under a big tree,","the cat and the rooster went up the tree, right to the top of it.","But before they fell asleep,","the rooster looked around, and not far away he caught sight of a firelight.","At once he called to his friends:","\"Listen, I see a firelight not far from here, there must be a house there.\"","Said the donkey:","\"Well then, let us get up and go there, for this is no comfortable place to sleep.\"","They did not think for long; they got themselves together and went toward the light.","As they went, the firelight grew bigger","and bigger, and all at once they were standing in front of the house.","It was a robbers' house!","The donkey, as the biggest of the band, went straight to the window and looked in.","\"Well, what do you see, old donkey?\"","asked the rooster.","\"Oh my, oh my! A table all laid, with food and drink on it, and so much of it!","Around the table sit robbers, eating, drinking, having a merry time.\"","\"How good it would be if we could sit down at the table too,\" said the rooster.","\"I believe you,\" agreed the donkey.","All of them put their heads together and talked it over,","how they could chase the robbers away.","And since they were all very clever, the talking did not last long.","Just listen to what they did.","The donkey put his two front hooves up on the window ledge,","the dog jumped up onto the donkey's back,","the cat onto the dog, and the rooster onto the very top of the cat's head.","When they were nicely settled like this, all at once they began to make music:","the donkey brayed, the dog barked,","the cat meowed, the rooster crowed,","and to this merry music they broke the window, and whoosh, into the house!","Oh, Holy Mary, Saint Joseph! How frightened the robbers were!","They thought ghosts were about,","so up they jumped from the table and ran head over heels out into the forest.","That was just what the musicians wanted; they sat down at the table, ate, drank,","played music, and grew so merry that they nearly kicked out the side of the house.","Toward midnight they grew sleepy, put out the fire and lay down.","The donkey went out into the farmyard and lay down at the foot of a haystack,","the hound stretched out behind the door,","the cat lay down on the hearth, and the rooster flew up onto the perch.","In a minute or two they were sleeping like logs, but the robbers were not sleeping:","from far off they watched in great fright to see what was happening in their house.","Then all at once they saw the firelight go out,","and the house went dark, and they thought: perhaps the ghosts have gone.","\"Go and look,\" said the leader to one of them, \"and let us see if they have gone.\"","The robber went off, groped his way into the room, and went straight to the hearth,","because he saw embers glowing there.","Ah, but it was the cat's eyes.","The robber did not know that, how could he have known?","He held a match to the cat's eyes.","Well, he could hold it there, the match did not light, but something else happened!","The cat leapt at the robber's face, hissed at him and scratched him all over.","Oh, how frightened the robber was! He ran off out of his wits,","but as he ran out through the door, the dog bit him in the leg,","and as he ran across the yard howling terribly,","the donkey by the haystack gave him a mighty kick in the side.","Only the rooster did not hurt him: he woke at the noise and shouted with all his might:","cock-a-doodle-doo, cock-a-doodle-dee! and he flapped his wings.","The robber ran on out of his wits and could hardly tell what had happened to him.","\"Oh, oh, let us run as far as our eyes can see, we cannot stay here.","On the hearth sits an old witch.","When I tried to light a fire, she blew in my eyes and scratched my face all over;","by the door stood a man, and he stabbed me in the leg with a big knife;","I ran into the yard, past the haystack,","and there some black monster struck me in the side with a club,","so hard that I will be a beggar from it all my life.","On the roof of the house sits the judge, and he kept shouting:","bring the robber here, bring him here!\"","Well, the robbers never dared even to look toward the house again; they ran","as if they had vanished from sight,","and the musicians stayed on in the house and lived merrily for as long as they had food.","And they are living still, if they have not died."]},
{"id":"kiraly_nyulai","title":"The King's Hares","src":"Hungarian tale, told by Benedek Elek","lines":["Once upon a time, beyond the seven times seven lands,","there lived a poor woman, and she had three sons.","They were as poor as church mice; they cooked with water","and stirred it with a stick, and lived from one day to the next.","The lads grew tired of this hard life, and they made up their minds","that they would go out from home somewhere,","for they could not go on like this forever and a day.","One day the eldest said to his mother:","\"Mother dear, bake me a cake in the ashes,","for I am setting out to try my luck.\"","The poor woman baked it, and the lad set out into the wide world.","On and on he went, over hills and valleys, and came to a golden well.","He sat down beside it, took out his ash cake, and began to eat.","As he sat there eating, a little mouse ran up to him and said:","\"Listen, lad, give me a bite of that cake,","for it is seven whole days since I have eaten a single bite.\"","\"I don't care,\" said the lad,","\"even if you have not eaten for seventy-seven days, as long as I have enough.\"","The little mouse crept away very sadly","and hid in a hole, and the lad went on his way.","He went and went until he came to the king's city.","He went straight to the palace, sat down by the gate, and waited for good luck.","Then all at once the king himself came out,","saw the lad, even greeted him, and asked:","\"What brings you here, poor lad?\"","\"Your Majesty, I put my life and death in your hands;","I am looking for work, if I can find any.\"","The king said:","\"Come in, lad. I have a hundred hares in my yard, and I give them to you.","Drive them out to the meadow, but guard them so well","that not one gets lost, or else it is the hares and your head!\"","Next morning the lad drove the hundred hares out,","but as soon as they reached the meadow, the hundred ran a hundred ways.","Oh, how the lad lost heart! He did not even dare look toward the palace;","he ran away and did not stop until he got home.","At home he told where he had been and all that had happened to him.","Then the second lad stepped forward","to try his luck, and he spat on his hands hard,","saying he would guard those hundred hares, heaven knows, if it took his life.","But he did just as \"well\" as the eldest.","At the golden well he too sat down to eat,","and the mouse asked him too for a bite of the ash cake, but he gave it none.","Then he went on to the king's city and took service with the king,","but the first morning he drove the hares out, the hundred ran a hundred ways.","He too went home very sadly, and now the youngest lad got ready to go:","perhaps luck would be kinder to him.","His mother baked him an ash cake as big","as a big cart wheel, and with it he set out into the world.","On and on he went, and came to the golden well.","As he sat there eating, the mouse ran up and asked him very nicely","to give it, for heaven's sake, a bite of cake,","for it was fourteen whole days since it had eaten.","\"Gladly, little mouse,\" said the lad,","\"perhaps the good Lord will help me, so I won't have to live long on this cake.\"","The little mouse thanked him kindly and said:","\"Well, poor lad, a good deed brings a good deed back!","I asked your two brothers, neither gave me anything, and the Lord helped neither.\"","With that it ran into its hole and came back with a little horn.","\"I give you this horn,\" said the mouse. \"Keep it, it may be useful.\"","\"Why, what could I ever do with this?\"","asked the lad.","\"Just take it,\" said the mouse, \"and whatever danger you are in,","blow it, and all that is bad will turn to good.\"","\"Well,\" thought the poor lad,","\"if it does no good, it does no harm. I'll take it with me.\"","He put it in his bag, went on, and did not stop until the king's city,","and in it, until the gate of the king's palace.","He arrived, sat down in the gateway, and soon the king came by.","\"What are you looking for here, poor lad?\"","asked the king.","The lad told him what he wanted: to find work in some good place.","\"You have come to the right place,\" said the king. \"I have a hundred hares,","and they need a clever herder, for I have not found one I like yet.\"","They shook hands at once, but the king warned the lad most strictly","that not one hare must be lost, or else it would be the hares and his head!","Very well.","Next morning the lad went out to the meadow with the hundred hares,","but before they even sniffed the grass, they ran off every which way.","\"Oh, Holy Mary, Saint Joseph!\" cried the lad. \"What shall I do now?\"","Then he remembered the horn; he pulled it out and blew it,","and the hundred hares turned at once and ran together like so many sheep.","The king saw this from the porch of the palace;","he shook his head and could not for the life of him think","what devilish trick the lad had used to gather those hares.","\"Just wait,\" he thought, \"today at least one of the hundred will be missing!","If not, I will put you to another test.\"","At once he ordered a servant girl to go out to the meadow with a sack","and ask the herder for a hare in the king's name.","The girl went out and asked for the hare, but the lad said","he would not give one even for heaven's sake, for his head was dear to him.","\"But this and that,\" said the girl, \"there is a feast at the house,","and the king will have both our heads off if there is no hare on the table.\"","As they argued like this, from who knows where","the little mouse appeared and whispered to the lad:","\"Just give her one, don't be afraid, and leave the rest to me!\"","The lad listened to the mouse, quickly grabbed a hare by the ears,","put it in the sack, and the girl set off with it.","That very minute the little mouse jumped up onto the sack,","and before the girl had taken a hundred steps, it chewed open the end of the sack;","the hare jumped out and ran back to the others as fast as it could go.","The mouse jumped down from the sack,","quickly picked up a big lump of cow dung and pushed it into the sack instead.","In the evening the lad went home and drove the hundred hares in at the big gate.","Out came the king to meet him, cursing so that the whole yard shook.","\"Come here, come here, you good-for-nothing! What did you send me in that sack?\"","The lad answered:","\"May God not be my God if it was not a hare, Your Majesty.\"","They called the servant girl.","\"Well, girl, what did the herder give you?\"","\"Truly, Your Majesty,","I put my life and death in your hands, he really gave me a hare.\"","\"Hm,\" said the king,","\"I never ate anything like that in all my life. So where is the hare now?\"","They counted the hares once, twice, three times, but it always came to a hundred.","\"Well, listen, poor lad,\" said the king,","\"I never had a herder like you, who sends a hare and still has none missing.","So what do you wish for?\"","\"Only a tub of money, Your Majesty, for we are very poor at home.\"","At once the king had a great big sack of money measured out,","so big that the lad needed twelve oxen to pull it home.","And they became so very rich that people came from far lands to marvel at them.","They are still alive today, if they have not died."]},
{"id":"acelgyuru","title":"The Steel Ring","src":"Hungarian tale, told by Benedek Elek","lines":["Once upon a time there was a poor man.","One day this poor man went into the forest for wood.","As he was picking up dry branches, he picked up a little snake with them.","He wanted to kill it, but the little snake spoke up and said:","\"Don't kill me, poor man,","for I will make you happy all your life if you spare my life.\"","\"Come now, what are you saying, little snake?\" said the poor man.","\"I am not such a silly fool as to believe your promise.\"","\"Oh, but do believe me, poor man, and you will not be sorry.","Here, I give you a steel ring. Put it on your finger,","and if you ever have a wish in your life,","just turn this ring around on your finger,","and I will be there at once and grant your wish.\"","\"So be it,\" thought the poor man. \"If it does no good,","it does no harm.\" He took the ring, put it on his finger, and went home.","He had hardly settled down at home","when he turned the ring on his finger, and there was the little snake.","\"What is your command, dear master?\"","\"My command is this,\" said the poor man,","\"by tomorrow morning, make me a palace here","such as not even the Turkish emperor has.\"","\"It shall be done, dear master,\" said the little snake,","and it vanished as if the earth had swallowed it.","Well, that evening the poor man went to bed and slept like a log.","When he woke in the morning, his eyes and mouth fell wide open,","for a shining palace had risen in place of his ragged little hut.","It was such as no human eye had ever seen in this world!","Everything around was pure gold, every single thing.","Even the table was gold,","and in its middle stood a dish of pure gold as big as a cart wheel.","\"Well,\" said the poor man to himself, \"that little snake did not trick me after all.","My, how wise I was to spare its life!\"","Again he turned the ring, and that very moment the little snake was there.","\"What is your command, dear master?\"","\"My command is that you bring me the Black King's daughter to be my wife.\"","\"It shall be done, dear master!\"","And as true as I live,","the next morning the Black King's daughter was there with him in a room.","And listen, she was such a wonderfully beautiful girl","that you could look at the sun, but not at her.","Oh, the poor man was happy; he lived like an emperor.","He got everything his heart desired;","he only had to give that steel ring one twist,","and the little snake was there and brought him everything.","A long, long time passed, and one day the poor man went out of that lovely palace.","He left home and forgot the steel ring there.","Just then an old junk dealer went past the palace, shouting at the top of his voice:","\"Rings for sale!","Rings!\"","The wife looked out and called him in,","and the poor thing gave him the steel ring for a gold ring, and paid extra too.","But somehow or other, the junk dealer knew the secret of the steel ring,","and as soon as he was out in the street, he turned it once.","That very moment the little snake was there.","It asked:","\"What is your command, dear master?\"","\"My command is that you lift up this palace, with the wife","and everything in it, and take it beyond the Operencian Sea.\"","\"It shall be done, dear master!\"","Oh dear good Lord, by the time the poor man came home,","only the empty ground was left of that dear, lovely, shining palace!","Ah! He grew very sad and set out into the world in sorrow,","hoping he might find his palace somewhere.","On and on he went, over hills and valleys, past the seven times seven lands.","As he reached the near shore of the Operencian Sea,","he saw a dog lying half dead, rolling about on the seashore.","It whimpered and whined bitterly and begged the poor man with dreadful howling","to heal it and not leave it there to die.","The poor man gathered all sorts of good herbs,","healed it with them, and the two went on together.","They had gone barely a good gunshot away when they found a sick cat.","It too begged the poor man to heal it.","Very well, he healed it, and now the three of them went on.","On and on they went along the seashore, and suddenly the poor man cried out:","\"Look, look there!","My palace is over on the far shore.","Well, somebody has moved that nicely!","Oh!","Oh!","Lord Jesus Christ, if by some wonder I could get across,","I would not take a mare with her foal for it.\"","The dog said:","\"Don't be sad, poor man!","You did me good, and a good deed brings a good deed back.","I will swim across the sea and think of something.\"","The cat said that it too would go across with the dog, even if it cost its life,","and would not close its eyes until the palace was back in its old place.","They did not take long to get ready: the dog jumped quickly into the sea,","the cat after it, climbed onto the dog's back, and so they swam across.","But so I don't get my words mixed up: the poor man told the dog clearly,","and the cat too, that there must be a steel ring in that palace.","They must get hold of it no matter what, and pay no heed to anything else.","Once they had the ring, they should hurry back, and the rest was his worry.","They swam across and went into the palace.","There lay the junk dealer asleep, with the ring tied to his beard.","That was all the cat needed.","It crept up to the bed, gently untied the ring, and then off they went,","as fast as they could! They ran out of the palace,","into the sea, and swam back very quickly.","But just when they reached the very middle of the sea, now listen","to what that wretched \"frog-cat\" of a cat did!","A fish swam by, the cat snapped at it, and dropped the ring into the sea.","Oh my goodness, what a to-do there was!","The dog wanted to drown the foolish cat in the sea,","but the cat begged so hard that the dog spared its life after all.","Well, what were they to do now, which way were they to turn?","They grieved terribly; they did not dare swim back without the ring.","They had to find it, even if it was at the bottom of the earth.","As they grieved like this, the cat caught a fish.","And this was the son of the king of the fishes!","The little fish begged:","\"Don't kill me, dear cat, I am the son of the fish king;","you will not be sorry if you spare my life!\"","They took it to the king of the fishes and told him what had happened.","They would spare his son's life if he found that ring,","for surely one of the fish had swallowed it.","The king of the fishes at once called a great meeting and asked every fish","whether it had a ring, but each one only answered","that none of its family had ever had a ring.","The dog grew very sad, and so did the cat,","and they were just about to leave when word came that one fish was still away.","They waited a little; perhaps that one had the ring.","And sure enough, a \"lame\" fish came, all in a sweat, and it brought the ring!","Oh, how glad the dog was, and the cat too!","The cat wanted to grab the ring, but the dog growled loudly:","\"Leave it, you! I won't trust you with it again!","You just curl up on my back, and I will take the ring in my mouth!\"","They did not even say \"bless you\", they ran off so fast.","They swam out to the shore and handed the ring to the poor man.","At once he put it on his finger, gave it a twist, and the little snake was there.","\"What is your command, dear master?\"","\"My command is this,\" said the poor man,","\"take that house back to its old place, and leave the junk dealer on the far shore.\"","\"It shall be done, dear master.\"","And it all happened as the poor man commanded.","The lovely palace came back, with the wife and everything in it,","to where it had first stood, and the poor man lived happily again.","He did not forget the dog and the cat either:","he bathed them in milk and butter, and gave them a log for a rub now and then.","It is true that ever since then they have been angry with each other,","but they are still alive, if they have not died."]},
{"id":"halasz","title":"The Fisherman and His Wife","src":"Brothers Grimm, told by Benedek Elek","lines":["Once upon a time there was a fisherman and his wife.","They lived in a little hut by the sea,","and they were very poor, for some days a fish took the hook and some days none did.","Well, what happened one day?","What happened was that a terribly big shark got caught on the fisherman's hook.","The fisherman pulled and pulled, but the fish spoke:","\"Spare my life! I am not a fish, I am an enchanted prince.","What good would I be to you if you caught me?","You could not eat my flesh anyway.\"","\"You are right,\" said the fisherman. \"Go with God, if you are truly not a real fish.\"","He let the fish go and went home with an empty bag.","\"Well now, did you catch no fish again?\"","asked his wife.","\"What will become of us?\"","\"Oh, I did catch one,\" said the fisherman,","\"I caught a terribly big shark, but it was not a real fish.","It said it was an enchanted prince, and it begged until I let it go.\"","\"Well, that was a foolish thing to do.","You should not have let it go until it granted you a wish.\"","\"And what would I have wished for?\"","\"Why, that it build us a proper house instead of this miserable hut.","Go right back, and if it gets caught on your hook once more,","do not let it go until it promises the house.\"","The fisherman only shook his head, for he thought it was a useless wish,","but he went back to the seashore and began to call:","\"Are you there, are you there,","Enchanted prince of the sea?","Do you hear, do you hear?","Come up from the sea to me.\"","At that very moment the sea gave a great splash,","it sloshed, and up popped the fish and asked the fisherman:","\"What do you want, old fisherman?\"","The fisherman answered:","\"A house is better than a hut.","This is what my wife would like:","A house to live in, not a hut.\"","Said the fish:","\"Go on home, old fisherman,","By morning the house will stand.\"","And sure enough, by morning a pretty little stone house stood there; they woke in it.","\"There, you see,\" said the wife, \"you only have to wish for something and it comes true.","Now go back and tell it that a pretty little stone house","is nice, very nice, but too small for us; it should build a palace instead.\"","\"Oh, wife, wife, what are you thinking?","This house is big enough for us.","What would you do in a palace?\"","\"Never you mind that, just go and ask for a palace!\"","\"The fish will be angry, wife, do not be silly!\"","\"Angry or not, just say what I tell you.","By tomorrow morning there must be a palace here, one as fine as the king's.\"","What could he do? The fisherman went and called the fish:","\"Are you there, are you there,","Enchanted prince of the sea?","Do you hear, do you hear?","Come up from the sea to me.\"","The fish came at once and asked:","\"What do you want, old fisherman?\"","The fisherman answered:","\"That house is too small for us.","This is what my wife would like:","A palace all her own to live in.\"","Said the fish:","\"Then go on home, nice and slow,","In the morning the palace will be ready.\"","The fisherman went home, they went to bed, and in the morning they woke in a palace,","a palace so huge that it had seventy-seven rooms,","and in the rooms were golden benches, tables, beds, and mirrors down to the floor.","Even the windows were pure gold, and so were the stairs.","Everywhere footmen and maids were bustling about;","not far from the palace stood a great big stable, full of horses with golden coats:","just as the fisherman and his wife stepped inside,","the grooms were combing them with golden combs and brushing them with golden brushes.","\"Well,\" said the wife, \"was it not a good thing that I wished for a palace?\"","\"Good, good,\" said the fisherman, \"now you cannot wish for anything more.\"","\"I will still think about that,\" said the wife.","The next morning the wife said:","\"This palace is fine, very fine, but it would only be truly fine if I were king.","Go to the fish and tell it that I want to be king.\"","\"Wife, wife, what are you saying?","I do not even dare to say that!\"","\"Why would you not dare?","Just go and tell it boldly that I want to be king.\"","The fisherman went off very sadly, for he did not like his wife's doings at all.","Now the enchanted prince would surely be angry, and how could he not be!","And indeed, the sea was not clear as at other times, it was grey,","it was dirty, as if it were angry too, and it roared and growled loudly.","But he feared his wife even more than the sea, more","than the enchanted prince, so he called out as before:","\"Are you there, are you there,","Enchanted prince of the sea?","Do you hear, do you hear?","Come up from the sea to me!\"","The sea gave a great slosh and splash, the fish popped up and asked the fisherman:","\"What is wrong, what is wrong, old fisherman?","Is the house perhaps not big enough?\"","The fisherman answered:","\"Oh, it is big enough, there is nothing wrong, nothing,","Only my wife wants to be king!\"","Said the fish:","\"If that is all she wishes for,","Then she shall be king, for sure!\"","Well, good Lord, by the time the fisherman got home, the palace had grown bigger,","and the yard too; guards stood in front of the palace,","generals walked up and down in the yard,","the army band played its music, the soldiers marched,","turning right, turning left,","and the fisherman's wife sat in the palace's biggest hall on a throne of pure gold,","guards stood before her, coming and going,","and the people of the court ran about and bowed before her.","\"Oh, my Lord!\" The fisherman clapped his hands. \"So you have really become king?\"","\"King indeed,\" said the wife.","\"Can you not see?","Here is the crown on my head.\"","\"Well, that is truly a fine thing.","With this you can be content until your dying day.\"","\"You might be content, but not I.","Go back to the fish at once and tell it that I want to be pope.\"","\"Oh wife, wife, what are you saying?","There is only one pope in the whole world, you cannot be that.\"","\"Not another word!\" the wife cried out,","\"Off with you, or I will have your head cut off at once!\"","The poor fisherman shivered all over at this terribly big wish.","But it was no use; still he went to the seashore and spoke to the fish:","\"Are you there, are you there,","Enchanted prince of the sea?","Do you hear, do you hear?","Come up from the sea to me.\"","Oh, but the sea was murky, so very murky, and it roared","and rumbled so that the poor fisherman covered his eyes and ears in fright.","Then all at once the fish leapt up, and you could see that it was angry now too.","It shouted at the fisherman:","\"Well, what do you want? Come here and tell,","Is being king not enough as well?\"","The fisherman answered:","\"Oh, it is not enough, not enough,","Now she would like to be pope!\"","\"Only pope?\"","said the shark,","\"So be it, let her be pope!\"","The fisherman ran home full of joy,","and by the royal palace stood a church so big that its towers (there were a thousand!)","nearly touched the sky. He went in, and there sat his wife on her throne,","kindly giving her blessing to the people, and all who could get near kissed her feet.","So the fisherman pushed his way up too, fell on his knees,","and kissed his wife's feet, and said as he did so:","\"Well, wife, now you have become pope as well,","you cannot be more than this, so now you can truly be content.\"","\"We shall see,\" said the wife.","All night she did not sleep, she kept tossing and turning,","and kept thinking about what else she could become.","\"Aha, I have it!\"","cried the wife.","\"I will become God.\"","She woke her husband and told him to run to the fish at once","and tell it her wish: she wanted to be God, being pope was not enough for her.","\"Wife, wife, you have lost your mind! What are you thinking?\"","\"What I am thinking is my business. Go, and be quick.\"","Oh, dear good God, the poor fisherman shivered twice before he reached the sea.","And even more when he saw the sea!","For whatever it had been before, now the sea roared and bellowed,","it swirled and tossed as if it wanted to swallow the whole world.","The poor fisherman just stood in one place and did not even dare to open his mouth.","But the fish came, it did not wait for a question, it asked in a terrible rage:","\"Is being pope not enough?","What more could she want now?\"","The fisherman answered, trembling, his teeth chattering:","\"Oh dear, what can I do?","Now she wants to be God!\"","Oh, the enchanted prince flew into a terrible rage,","his eyes threw sparks, and he roared so loud the poor fisherman fell over in fright.","\"What does the silly old goose want?","Back to the hut with her, go!\"","At that very moment the sea covered everything, simply everything,","and the next morning, when it had gone back into its bed,","there was nothing on the seashore but the little hut,","and in front of it sat the fisherman and his wife, very sad, just like before.","They are still alive today, if they have not died."]},
{"id":"szerencses_janos","title":"Lucky John","src":"Brothers Grimm, told by Benedek Elek","lines":["Once upon a time there was a lad, and his name was John.","This John lived with a farmer for seven whole years,","and when the seventh year was over, he said to his master:","\"Master, my time is up, now I am going home to my dear mother: please pay me my wages.\"","\"Very well, John,\" said the farmer,","\"you have been a good lad, you served me honestly, here are your wages.\"","He gave him a lump of gold as big","as John's head, and even a little bigger than that.","John tied it up in a sack, put it on his shoulder and set off for home.","As he walked along, a man on horseback came toward him, and John began to sigh:","\"Oh my, what a fine thing riding is!","The rider sits on his horse just like on a chair, he does not stumble on the stones,","he does not wear out his boots, and still he moves along.\"","The rider heard this and said to John:","\"Well then, why do you go on foot?\"","\"Because,\" said John, \"I have no horse.","And I must carry this gold home: I served seven years for it!","Oh, but it is heavy, my shoulder is about to break.\"","\"Do you know what?","Let us trade,\" said the rider.","\"I will give you the horse, and you give me the gold.\"","\"Gladly,\" said John, and in that very moment he threw the sack off his shoulder.","The rider got off the horse, helped John up onto it, and even gave him good advice:","\"Now, if you want to go fast, just urge it on:","gee up, gee up! and it will run like the swift wind.\"","Well, they wished each other God's blessing, parted, and went their two ways.","Oh, how happy John was!","Now he had a horse.","And what a horse!","He tugged at the reins and urged the horse on: gee up,","gee up! and the horse really did dash off, so fast","that John tipped now to the left, now to the right, and he tipped this way","and that, until all at once, bump! he tumbled into the ditch.","The poor fellow could hardly scramble up, his bones creaked,","every limb ached, and he was very sorry he had traded the gold for a horse.","Just as he got up, a man came along that way, driving a cow.","This man caught the horse and asked John:","\"What is wrong, little brother?","The horse threw you off, did it not?\"","\"Oh, it did, may the wolves eat it!","Well, I will never sit on this nasty beast again.","Oh, a cow is so much better than a horse!","You can stroll quietly behind it, and it gives you milk and butter too.\"","\"Do you know what,\" said the farmer, \"let us trade.\"","\"Gladly,\" said John.","\"Better your neck gets broken than mine!\"","\"Do not you worry about that,\" said the farmer,","and with that he jumped onto the horse and galloped off like the swift wind.","John set off with the cow too, and they ambled along nice and slowly.","Oh my, how happy John was!","Now he would have no worries for the rest of his life.","If he had bread, he would have milk and butter, and cheese if he liked: what more, John?","On the way he stopped at an inn, and there he ate","what bread he had with him; he had a few pennies, so he drank some wine too,","then in a merry mood he set off again, and the two of them walked on,","they walked and walked, ambling slowly toward home.","It was getting near noon, and the heat was stifling,","and John grew so faint with thirst that his tongue hung out.","He thought to himself: here is the cow, I will milk it and my thirst will be gone.","He tied the cow to a tree, and since he had no pail with him (where would he get one?),","he took his cap, held it under the cow's udder and began to milk.","He milked and milked, but not a single drop of milk came out.","And because he wanted milk no matter what, he squeezed and tugged hard at the udder,","the cow grew tired of the joke and kicked John in the head so hard","that he fell flat on his back, lay sprawled out, and even fainted, the poor lad.","When at long last he woke up, he did not know where he was: in the sky or on the ground.","Luckily, a butcher came along that way, pushing a piglet in his wheelbarrow.","He stopped and asked John:","\"What is wrong, little brother?\"","John told him what had happened.","The butcher held out his flask and offered him a drink, to help him come round.","Then he looked at the cow, shook his head and said:","\"You can milk this one all you like, little brother, there is not a drop of milk in her.","She is an old beast, she will never give milk again in all this world.\"","Oh, how sad John became!","\"Well, who would have thought it,\" he grumbled to himself.","\"What shall I do now?","Have her killed?","Who will eat the meat of this old beast?\"","\"Do you know what,\" said the butcher, \"I will help you out of your trouble.","I will give you this piglet, and you give me the cow,","I will cut up her meat and somehow sell it with the rest of my meat.\"","\"Oh, God bless you, sir!\" John thanked him.","They took the piglet off the wheelbarrow,","tied a rope to its hind leg, and John set off with it.","He could hardly contain himself, he was bursting with joy to see","how lucky he was, what a good bargain he had made again!","So on they went, walking and walking.","All at once a young lad joined him, carrying a fine white goose under his arm.","They fell to talking; John told him of his great good luck,","and the lad told him he was taking the goose to a christening feast.","\"Just lift it, feel how heavy it is,\" said the lad.","\"No wonder, we fattened it for eight weeks.","Well, whoever eats this will be licking his lips afterwards, I know.\"","John took the goose in his hands and lifted it.","\"It really is good and heavy,\" he said, \"but my piglet is no small thing either!\"","Meanwhile the lad looked around worriedly, shook his head and hummed.","Then at last he spoke up:","\"Listen, my friend, you are going to get into trouble with this piglet.\"","\"Why would I get into trouble?\"","\"Because I just heard in the village,","the one I came through, that someone stole a piglet from the mayor.","People are searching for it everywhere.","What if this is the stolen piglet?","I tell you truly, you will end up in prison.\"","Oh, how frightened John was!","He began to beg the lad to trade with him.","He would gladly give him the piglet for the goose, if only he could keep out of trouble.","The lad made him beg for a while, then, so be it, he gave John the goose,","and with the piglet he quickly turned onto a side road and swiftly vanished.","John went on his way too, turning the trade over in his mind.","\"If I think it over well,\" he said to himself, \"I made a good trade.","For one thing, this fat goose has plenty of fat; for another,","it has feathers too, we will stuff a pillow with them. Oh, how glad my mother will be!\"","So John walked on and on, and when he reached the last village,","he saw an old knife grinder there, grinding busily and singing all the while:","\"Round and round the wheel goes,","Money in my pocket grows.","I just grind the knives and shears,","Coins jingle in my pocket here!\"","John stopped, greeted the grinder and said:","\"Oh, you must have a good life, sir, to be in such a merry mood!\"","\"I should say so,\" answered the grinder.","\"No man on earth has it better than a knife grinder.","He just grinds and grinds, and his pocket never empties, it is always full of money.\"","\"Is that really so?\"","\"It is really so.","And where did you buy that fine goose?\"","\"I did not buy it, I traded it for a piglet.\"","\"For a piglet?","Is that really so?\"","\"It is really so.\"","\"And where did you get the piglet?\"","\"I got it for a cow.\"","\"Is that really so?\"","\"It is really so.\"","\"And the cow?\"","\"I got that for a horse.\"","\"Is that really so?\"","\"It is really so.\"","\"And the horse?\"","\"I got that for a lump of gold as big as my head.\"","\"Is that really so?\"","\"It is really so.","I could even swear to it.\"","\"Well, listen,\" said the grinder, \"I have seen many lucky men,","but never one like you, and I have grown old and eaten the best of my bread.\"","\"I should say so!\"","\"You see, it is a pity you are not a knife grinder.","Then you would have lots of money.\"","\"That is true,\" said John, \"but I have no grindstone.\"","\"Do you know what, I will give you a grindstone.","You can make a start with that.","And you give me the goose.\"","\"Gladly, old man!\" cried John with great joy.","They shook hands on it right there, and the grinder gave him a worn,","chipped grindstone, and then picked up a big stone off the ground and gave him that too:","\"Here, my son, take it,","you can hammer bent nails straight with it. Use it in good health.\"","Well, how happy he was!","\"I truly was born lucky,\" John said to himself as he walked along.","But all at once he grew weary, the stone grew heavier","and heavier, he was hungry and had nothing to eat;","he was thirsty and had nothing to drink. Oh dear, oh dear,","he would not have minded at all to be rid of the stones.","By blessed luck he came to a well,","there he threw down the stones and bent over the well to drink from it.","As he bent over, somehow or other, both stones went splash into the well.","\"Hooray! How good!","Oh, how good!\"","John jumped up with joy.","\"I do not have to lug them home, oh, how good!","Well, there has never been a man in the world as lucky as I am!\"","He did not stop until he got home, he ran all the way to his dear mother.","Tomorrow let John be your guest."]}];
